e MODros Inf Serv. vegetation indices.Remote Sens Environ.2002;83:195–213.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0034-4257(02)00096-2)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.1016%2fS0034-4257(02)00096-2)\n- \n\nHijmansRJ,CameronSE,ParraJL,JonesPG,JarvisA.Very high resolution interpolated climate surfaces for global land areas.Int J Climatol.2005;25:1965–78.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1002/joc.1276)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.1002%2fjoc.1276)\n- \n\nNovellaNS,ThiawWM.African rainfall climatology version 2 for famine early warning systems.J Appl Meteorol Climatol.2013;52:588–606.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1175/JAMC-D-11-0238.1)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.1175%2fJAMC-D-11-0238.1)\n- \n\nValentiniG,DietterichTG. Low bias bagged support vector machines. In: Proceedings of the 20th International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML-2003), Washington DC;2003. p. 752–9 [cited 2014 Dec 15].[http://www.aaai.org/Papers/ICML/2003/ICML03-098.pdf](http://www.aaai.org/Papers/ICML/2003/ICML03-098.pdf)\n- \n\nBreimanL.Bagging predictors.Mach Learn.1996;24:123–40.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00058655)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.1007%2fBF00058655)\n- \n\nPetersonAT,BauerJT,MillsJN.Ecologic and geographic distribution of filovirus disease.Emerg Infect Dis.2004;10:40–7.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.3201/eid1001.030125)[PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15078595)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.3201%2feid1001.030125&pmid=15078595)\n- \n\nAltizerS,BartelR,HanBA.Animal migration and infectious disease risk.Science.2011;331:296–302.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1194694)[PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21252339)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.1126%2fscience.1194694&pmid=21252339)\n- \n\nAltizerS,DobsonA,HosseiniP,HudsonP,PascualM,RohaniP.Seasonality and the dynamics of infectious diseases.Ecol Lett.2006;9:467–84.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1461-0248.2005.00879.x)[PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16623732)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.1111%2fj.1461-0248.2005.00879.x&pmid=16623732)\n- \n\nPetersonAT,PapeşM,CarrollDS,LeirsH,JohnsonKM.Mammal taxa constituting potential coevolved reservoirs of filoviruses.J Mammal.2007;88:1544–54.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1644/06-MAMM-A-280R1.1)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.1644%2f06-MAMM-A-280R1.1)\n- \n\nHaymanDT,YuM,CrameriG,WangLF,Suu-IreR,WoodJL,et al.Ebola virus antibodies in fruit bats, Ghana, West Africa.Emerg Infect Dis.2012;18:1207–9.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.3201/eid1807.111654)[PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22710257)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.3201%2feid1807.111654&pmid=22710257)\n- \n\nFormentyP,BoeschC,WyersM,SteinerC,DonatiF,DindF,et al.Ebola virus outbreak among wild chimpanzees living in a rain forest of Côte d’Ivoire.J Infect Dis.1999;179(Suppl 1):S120–6.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1086/514296)[PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9988175)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.1086%2f514296&pmid=9988175)\n- \n\nFormentyP,HatzC,Le GuennoB,StollA,RogenmoserP,WidmerA.Human infection due to Ebola virus, subtype Côte d’Ivoire: clinical and biologic presentation.J Infect Dis.1999;179(Suppl 1):S48–53.[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1086/514285)[PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9988164)[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?doi=10.1086%2f514285&pmid=9988164)\n\n[Top](javascript:void(0))\n\n##### Figures\n- [**Figure 1**. Locations of known Ebola virus spillover events, Africa, 1960–2010. Light-shaded area indicates the focal region in Africa of annual rainfall >500 mm. Open circles indicate human spillovers, open triangles...](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/3/16-0101-f1)\n- [**Figure 2**. Seasonal spatiotemporal dynamics of Ebola virus spillover intensity (i.e., average density or expected number of points per unit area and month) as percentile values ranking predicted intensities at all...](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/3/16-0101-f2)\n- [**Figure 3**. Phase graph showing the relationship between mean monthly rainfall and raw Ebola spillover intensity (defined as average density or expected number of points per unit area and/or time) for...](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/3/16-0101-f3)\n- [**Figure 4**. Change in annual Ebola spillover intensity (defined as average density or expected number of points per unit area and time), Africa, 1975–2015. Warm colors indicate increased spillover intensity; cool...](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/3/16-0101-f4)\n- [**Figure 5**. Change in human population size (log10/25 km2 grid cell), Africa, 1975–2015. Warm colors indicate increased population size, cool colors population declines. Dotted horizontal line marks the equator.](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/3/16-0101-f5)\n- [**Video**. Seasonal spatiotemporal dynamics of Ebola virus spillover intensity (i.e., average density or expected number of points per unit area and month) as percentile values ranking predicted intensities at...](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/3/16-0101-vid1)\n\n[Top](javascript:void(0))[Cite This Article](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/3/16-0101_article)\n\nDOI: 10.3201/eid2303.160101\n\n[Table of Contents – Volume 23, Number 3—March 2017](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/articles/issue/23/3/table-of-contents)\n\nEID Search Options[**Advanced Article Search**](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/AdvancedSearch)– Search articles by author and/or keyword.[**Articles by Country Search**](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/ArticleMap)– Search articles by the topic country.[**Article Type Search**](https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/ArticleTypeSearch)– Search articles by article type and issue.\n\n[Top](javascript:void(0))\n\nComments\n\nPlease use the form below to submit correspondence to the authors or contact them at the following address:\n\nJohn Paul Schmidt, University of Georgia, Odum School of Ecology, 140 E Green St, Athens, GA 30602, USA\n\n**Comment submitted successfully, thank you for your feedback.**\n\n**There was an unexpected error. Message not sent.**\n\n[Top](javascript:void(0))\n\nPage created: February 24, 2017\n\nPage updated: February 24, 2017\n\nPage reviewed: February 24, 2017\n\n*The conclusions, findings, and opinions expressed by authors contributing to this journal do not necessarily reflect the official position of the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, the Public Health Service, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, or the authors' affiliated institutions. Use of trade names is for identification only and does not imply endorsement by any of the groups named above.*\n\n#### Article Citations\n\nHighlight and copy the desired format.EIDSchmidt J, Park AW, Kramer AM, Han BA, Alexander LW, Drake JM. Spatiotemporal Fluctuations and Triggers of Ebola Virus Spillover. Emerg Infect Dis. 2017;23(3):415-422. https://doi.org/10.3201/eid2303.160101AMASchmidt J, Park AW, Kramer AM, et al. Spatiotemporal Fluctuations and Triggers of Ebola Virus Spillover.*Emerging Infectious Diseases*. 2017;23(3):415-422. doi:10.3201/eid2303.160101.APASchmidt, J., Park, A. W., Kramer, A. M., Han, B. A., Alexander, L. W., & Drake, J. M. (2017). Spatiotemporal Fluctuations and Triggers of Ebola Virus Spillover.*Emerging Infectious Diseases*,*23*(3), 415-422. https://doi.org/10.3201/eid2303.160101.\n\n#### Metric Details\n\n#### Spatiotemporal Fluctuations and Triggers of Ebola Virus Spillover\n\nVolume 23, Number 3—March 2017\n\n#### Article Views: 2471\n\nData is collected weekly and does not include downloads and attachments. View data is from.Time periodFirst 3 MonthsLast 3 MonthsLast YearLast 2 YearsAll Data\n\n#### Citations:54\n\n#### Altmetrics\n\n*Click a source for Altmetric details*\n\n##### **What is the Altme